5380859375, -0.036834716796875, -0.007518768310546875, 0.016021728515625, 0.00711822509765625, 0.0323486328125, -0.003719329833984375, -0.00797271728515625, 0.0017480850219726562, -0.0059661865234375, -0.0131072998046875, -0.0034198760986328125, 0.0032901763916015625, 0.0205078125, -0.0054473876953125, -0.02099609375, 0.004749298095703125, 0.00977325439453125, -0.01727294921875, 0.0240631103515625, 0.01568603515625, -0.00562286376953125, 0.0181427001953125, -0.01007080078125, 0.03643798828125, -0.0162811279296875, -0.006687164306640625, 0.00048613548278808594, 0.0103912353515625, -0.0224456787109375, -0.0015544891357421875, 0.01947021484375, -0.02960205078125, 0.0001556873321533203, -0.024261474609375, -0.004878997802734375, 0.0012359619140625, 0.01641845703125, 0.00811004638671875, -0.049957275390625, 0.037200927734375, -0.0180816650390625, -0.01056671142578125, -0.006351470947265625, 0.006107330322265625, -0.00862884521484375, -0.0106964111328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**1. Introduction and Definitions**\n\nWe investigate the relationship between weak convergence of synaptic input measures and the firing behavior of sparse threshold-reset networks in the fast-synapse limit. Throughout, we use the following definitions:\n\n- *Componentwise weak convergence*: For a sequence of signed synaptic measures $(\\mu_N^+, \\mu_N^-)$ (excitatory and inhibitory), we say they converge componentwise weakly to $(\\mu^+, \\mu^-)$ if, for every bounded continuous test function $f$, $\\int f\\,d\\mu_N^\\pm \\to \\int f\\,d\\mu^\\pm$ as $N\\to\\infty$.\n- *Fast-synapse limit*: The regime where all synaptic kernel delays $\\varepsilon_N$ tend to zero, i.e., $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow 0$ as $N\\to\\infty$.\n- *Causal event protocol*: A spike processing protocol in which spikes are handled in strict temporal order, with refractoriness enforced by clamping the membrane potential after a spike, and synaptic kernels are smooth, compactly supported functions with strictly positive delays.\n- *Typewise-mixing*: A property of random graphs where, for each synaptic class, the subgraph induced by that class is sufficiently mixing so that the empirical distribution of incoming edges per neuron converges to the class distribution.\n\n**2. Setup: Spike Train Construction**\n\nWe construct two families of input spike trains for a target neuron, parameterized by a small delay $\\varepsilon>0$ and pulse masses $a>0$ (excitatory), $b>0$ (inhibitory):\n\n- *Excitatory-first*: The neuron receives an excitatory spike at time $t=0$ and an inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n- *Inhibitory-first*: The neuron receives an inhibitory spike at $t=0$ and an excitatory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n\nBoth spikes are modeled as smooth, nonnegative, compactly supported kernels $K_\\varepsilon(t)$ with support in $[0,\\varepsilon]$ and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. The excitatory (resp. inhibitory) input is $a K_\\varepsilon(t)$ (resp. $-b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)$, with $\\delta=0$ or $\\varepsilon$). As $\\varepsilon\\downarrow 0$, both input measures converge weakly to $a\\delta_0$ and $-b\\delta_0$.\n\n**3. Main Result: Firing Behavior and Robustness**\n\n*Theorem 1 (Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit).*  \nLet the neuron have initial state $x$ and threshold $\\theta$. In the fast-synapse limit $\\varepsilon\\downarrow 0$, the neuron fires in the excitatory-first case but not in the inhibitory-first case if and only if $x+a-b<\\theta\\le x+a$.\n\n*Robustness*: This firing outcome persists under perturbations of the initial state $x$ by at most $\\eta>0$, pulse masses $a,b$ by at most $\\eta$, and bounded drift $|dx/dt|\\le \\eta$, provided $\\theta$ is at least $2\\eta$ away from the endpoints $x+a-b$ and $x+a$. That is, for all $|\\delta x|, |\\delta a|, |\\delta b| \\le \\eta$ and $|dx/dt|\\le \\eta$, the firing outcome is unchanged.\n\n*Proof Sketch*: The neuron\u2019s membrane potential after both pulses is $x+a-b$ in both cases, but the order of arrival determines whether the threshold is crossed before inhibition arrives. The robustness follows by continuity and the strict threshold margin.\n\n**4. Propagation to Sparse Networks**\n\nWe consider a random block graph $G_N$ with $N$ neurons, each with $q_N$ outgoing edges ($q_N\\to\\infty$, $q_N/N\\to 0$), partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes. *Dale compatibility* means each neuron is either purely excitatory or inhibitory. The network is initialized identically in both the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first protocols, with the only difference being the order of paired spikes as above.\n\n*Propagation Mechanism*: For each neuron, the probability that the excitatory spike arrives before the inhibitory one is $1/2$ in the limit, due to random edge assignments and independent delays. Thus, the expected difference in firing counts between the two protocols is $1/2$ per neuron. More precisely, letting $F_N^{(E)}$ and $F_N^{(I)}$ be the total firing counts in the excitatory-first and inhibitory-first systems, we have\n\\[\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\frac{1}{N}(F_N^{(E)} - F_N^{(I)})\\right] \\to \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{N}(F_N^{(E)} - F_N^{(I)}) = \\frac{1}{2} + o_{L^1}(1)\n\\]\nwhere $o_{L^1}(1)$ denotes convergence to zero in $L^1$ as $N\\to\\infty$. This macroscopic difference persists after reset, as shown by constructing a bounded-degree subnetwork and probing at later times.\n\n**5. Mean-Field Regime and Convergence Rates**\n\nFor fixed positive-delay kernels and finitely many synaptic classes, we consider directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs with mean degree $\\lambda_N\\to\\infty$, $\\lambda_N/N\\to 0$, and empirical class distribution $\\pi_N$ converging to $\\pi$. In the *typewise-mixing* regime, before any neuron\u2019s threshold is grazed, the network dynamics converge to a delayed class mean-field system:\n\n*Theorem 2 (Mean-Field Convergence).*  \nLet $X_N(t)$ denote the empirical measure of neuron states at time $t$. Then, for $t$ before the first threshold crossing,\n\\[\n\\|X_N(t) - \\bar{X}(t)\\| \\le O_P(\\lambda_N^{-1/2} + \\|\\pi_N - \\pi\\|_1)\n\\]\nwhere $\\bar{X}(t)$ is the solution to the class-averaged mean-field equation, and $O_P$ denotes stochastic boundedness in probability.\n\n*Proof Sketch*: The result follows from concentration inequalities for sparse random graphs and standard mean-field arguments, using the mixing property to control deviations.\n\n**6. Discussion and Future Directions**\n\nOur results delineate two regimes: (1) In the stable averaging regime (fixed delay, typewise-mixing), macroscopic behavior is determined by class-averaged inputs and is insensitive to microscopic timing. (2) In the singular collapse regime (fast-synapse limit), componentwise weak convergence fails to capture the signed arrival-order information required by the threshold-reset mechanism, leading to macroscopic discrepancies.\n\n*Limitations and Future Work*: Weak convergence alone is insufficient for predicting network responses in the fast-synapse limit. A promising direction is to consider *ordered measure convergence* (e.g., convergence in the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology for cumulative input processes) or to track the joint law of input arrival times. Formalizing such notions may yield convergence criteria that retain arrival-order information and better predict network-level computations.\n\n**Acknowledgments and References**\n\nFor formal definitions and proofs, see [1] (for weak convergence), [2] (mean-field limits in sparse graphs), and [3] (threshold-reset dynamics).  \n[1] Billingsley, P. (1999). *Convergence of Probability Measures*.  \n[2] Delarue, F., Inglis, J., Rubenthaler, S., & Tanr\u00e9, E. (2015). *Global solvability of a networked integrate-and-fire model of McKean\u2013Vlasov type*. Ann. Appl. Probab.  \n[3] Gerstner, W., Kistler, W. M., Naud, R., & Paninski, L. (2014). *Neuronal Dynamics*."}}